=====================================
  init-tables.sql — Detailed Explanation
=====================================

This script initializes the PostgreSQL schema for the blogger-platform application.
It creates a reusable trigger function, 6 tables, associated indexes, and triggers.


========================================================
1. TRIGGER FUNCTION: update_updated_at_column()
========================================================

    CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_updated_at_column()
    RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$
    BEGIN
        NEW.updated_at = NOW();
        RETURN NEW;
    END;
    $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

Purpose:
  A reusable PL/pgSQL trigger function that automatically sets the `updated_at`
  column to the current timestamp whenever a row is updated. This eliminates the
  need for the application layer to manually manage this field on every UPDATE.

How it works:
  - `NEW` refers to the new version of the row being updated.
  - `NOW()` returns the current transaction timestamp (TIMESTAMPTZ).
  - `RETURN NEW;` passes the modified row onward so the UPDATE proceeds.
  - `CREATE OR REPLACE` makes the script idempotent — safe to run multiple times.

This function is attached to every table via individual BEFORE UPDATE triggers.


========================================================
2. TABLE: users
========================================================

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
        id                                  UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        login                               VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
        password_hash                       VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
        email                               VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
        first_name                          VARCHAR(255) DEFAULT NULL,
        last_name                           VARCHAR(255) DEFAULT NULL,
        email_confirmation_code             VARCHAR(255) DEFAULT NULL,
        email_confirmation_expiration_date  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NULL,
        email_is_confirmed                  BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE,
        password_recovery_code              VARCHAR(255) DEFAULT NULL,
        password_recovery_expiration_date   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NULL,
        created_at                          TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at                          TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        deleted_at                          TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NULL
    );

Columns:
  - id (UUID, PK)               — Auto-generated UUID v4 primary key.
  - login (VARCHAR(10))         — User's login name. Max 10 characters.
  - password_hash (VARCHAR(255))— Bcrypt hash of the user's password.
  - email (VARCHAR(255))        — User's email address.
  - first_name (VARCHAR(255))   — Optional first name (nullable).
  - last_name (VARCHAR(255))    — Optional last name (nullable).
  - email_confirmation_code     — UUID/random code sent to verify email. Nullable
                                  (NULL once confirmed or before registration email sent).
  - email_confirmation_expiration_date — When the confirmation code expires. Nullable.
  - email_is_confirmed (BOOLEAN)— Whether the user's email has been verified.
                                  Defaults to FALSE.
  - password_recovery_code      — Code sent for password reset flow. Nullable.
  - password_recovery_expiration_date — When the recovery code expires. Nullable.
  - created_at (TIMESTAMPTZ)    — Row creation timestamp, auto-set to NOW().
  - updated_at (TIMESTAMPTZ)    — Last modification timestamp, auto-updated by trigger.
  - deleted_at (TIMESTAMPTZ)    — Soft delete timestamp. NULL means the user is active.

Indexes:

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_login_unique
      ON users (login) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

    Partial unique index — ensures login uniqueness only among non-deleted users.
    This allows a deleted user's login to be reused by a new registration.

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email_unique
      ON users (email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

    Same pattern for email uniqueness. Deleted users don't block email reuse.

  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_confirmation_code
      ON users (email_confirmation_code)
      WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND email_confirmation_code IS NOT NULL;

    Partial index for looking up users by confirmation code during the email
    verification flow. The double WHERE condition keeps the index small — it
    only includes active users who actually have a pending confirmation code.

  CREATE INDEX idx_users_password_recovery_code
      ON users (password_recovery_code)
      WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND password_recovery_code IS NOT NULL;

    Same pattern for password recovery code lookup. Only indexes rows with
    an active recovery code, keeping the index minimal.

Trigger:

  CREATE TRIGGER trigger_users_updated_at
      BEFORE UPDATE ON users
      FOR EACH ROW
      EXECUTE FUNCTION update_updated_at_column();

    Fires before every UPDATE on the users table, automatically refreshing
    the updated_at timestamp.

Design decisions:
  - Email confirmation and password recovery fields are flattened directly into
    the users table rather than separate tables. In the MongoDB version these were
    embedded sub-documents (schemas). Flattening avoids JOINs for these frequently
    accessed fields and mirrors the original document structure.
  - Soft delete via `deleted_at` allows preserving user data for audit/recovery
    while logically removing the user from the application.
  - Partial unique indexes are the PostgreSQL-idiomatic way to enforce uniqueness
    only among active (non-deleted) records.


