3. TABLE: security_devices
========================================================

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS security_devices (
        id                UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        user_id           UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
        device_id         VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL UNIQUE,
        ip                VARCHAR(45) NOT NULL,
        title             VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
        last_active_date  INTEGER NOT NULL,
        expiration_date   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
        created_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        deleted_at        TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NULL
    );

Columns:
  - id (UUID, PK)               — Auto-generated primary key.
  - user_id (UUID, FK → users)  — The user who owns this session/device.
  - device_id (VARCHAR(255))    — Unique identifier for the device/session, used
                                  in refresh tokens to bind tokens to devices.
  - ip (VARCHAR(45))            — IP address of the device. VARCHAR(45) accommodates
                                  the longest possible IPv6 representation.
  - title (VARCHAR(255))        — User-agent or device name/description.
  - last_active_date (INTEGER)  — Unix timestamp (seconds) of last activity. Stored
                                  as INTEGER rather than TIMESTAMPTZ, matching the
                                  application-level representation used in JWT iat claims.
  - expiration_date (TIMESTAMPTZ)— When this session/refresh token expires.
  - created_at, updated_at, deleted_at — Standard audit and soft delete columns.

Indexes:

  CREATE INDEX idx_security_devices_user_id
      ON security_devices (user_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

    Partial index for finding all active sessions belonging to a user.
    Used when listing devices or terminating all other sessions.

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_security_devices_device_id_unique
      ON security_devices (device_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

    Partial unique index ensuring device_id uniqueness among active sessions.
    Note: there's also a table-level UNIQUE constraint on device_id (line 59),
    which is redundant with this partial index. The table-level constraint enforces
    uniqueness across ALL rows (including soft-deleted ones), while the partial
    index only enforces it for active rows.

Trigger:
  Same BEFORE UPDATE trigger pattern for auto-updating updated_at.

Design decisions:
  - Foreign key to users(id) without ON DELETE CASCADE — the application handles
    deletion logic via soft deletes, not database cascades.
  - last_active_date as INTEGER (Unix timestamp) aligns with JWT `iat` claim format,
    avoiding conversion between application and database layers.


