5. TABLE: posts
========================================================

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS posts (
        id                UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        title             VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,
        short_description VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
        content           VARCHAR(1000) NOT NULL,
        blog_id           UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES blogs(id),
        blog_name         VARCHAR(15) NOT NULL,
        likes_count       INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
        dislikes_count    INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
        created_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at        TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        deleted_at        TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NULL
    );

Columns:
  - id (UUID, PK)              — Auto-generated primary key.
  - title (VARCHAR(30))        — Post title. Max 30 characters.
  - short_description (VARCHAR(100)) — Brief summary of the post.
  - content (VARCHAR(1000))    — Full post content.
  - blog_id (UUID, FK → blogs) — The blog this post belongs to.
  - blog_name (VARCHAR(15))    — Denormalized blog name (see design decisions below).
  - likes_count (INTEGER)      — Denormalized count of "Like" reactions. Defaults to 0.
  - dislikes_count (INTEGER)   — Denormalized count of "Dislike" reactions. Defaults to 0.
  - created_at, updated_at, deleted_at — Standard audit and soft delete columns.

Indexes:

  CREATE INDEX idx_posts_blog_id
      ON posts (blog_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;

    Partial index for finding all active posts belonging to a blog. Used in
    the "get posts by blog ID" endpoint.

Trigger:
  Same BEFORE UPDATE trigger pattern for auto-updating updated_at.

Design decisions:
  - blog_name is DENORMALIZED — duplicated from the blogs table into posts. This
    avoids a JOIN when listing posts (the API response includes the blog name).
    The trade-off is that if a blog's name changes, the application must also update
    all related posts' blog_name values.
  - likes_count and dislikes_count are DENORMALIZED counters. Instead of computing
    these via COUNT(*) queries against the likes table on every read, the application
    updates these counters when likes are created/changed. This is a classic read
    optimization — fast reads at the cost of slightly more complex write logic.


========================================================
6. TABLE: comments
========================================================

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS comments (
        id                      UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        content                 VARCHAR(300) NOT NULL,
        commentator_user_id     UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
        commentator_user_login  VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL,
        post_id                 UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id),
        created_at              TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at              TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()
    );

Columns:
  - id (UUID, PK)                      — Auto-generated primary key.
  - content (VARCHAR(300))             — Comment text. Max 300 characters.
  - commentator_user_id (UUID, FK → users) — The user who wrote the comment.
  - commentator_user_login (VARCHAR(10))   — Denormalized user login (see below).
  - post_id (UUID, FK → posts)         — The post this comment belongs to.
  - created_at, updated_at             — Audit timestamps.

Notable: NO deleted_at column. Comments use hard deletes (or are never deleted
individually), unlike other entities in this schema.

Indexes:

  CREATE INDEX idx_comments_post_id
      ON comments (post_id);

    Non-partial index (no WHERE clause) for finding all comments on a post.
    Since comments lack deleted_at, there's no need for a partial index.

Trigger:
  Same BEFORE UPDATE trigger pattern for auto-updating updated_at.

Design decisions:
  - commentator_user_login is DENORMALIZED from the users table. The API response
    includes the commenter's login, so storing it directly avoids a JOIN. In the
    MongoDB version this was an embedded sub-document (commentatorInfo).
  - No soft delete — comments table has no deleted_at column. This is a deliberate
    design choice; when comments need to be removed, the testing endpoint truncates
    the whole table. Individual comment deletion may simply delete the row.


========================================================
7. TABLE: likes
========================================================

    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS likes (
        id          UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        user_id     UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
        parent_id   UUID NOT NULL,
        parent_type VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL CHECK (parent_type IN ('comment', 'post')),
        status      VARCHAR(10) NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('None', 'Like', 'Dislike')),
        created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()
    );

Columns:
  - id (UUID, PK)           — Auto-generated primary key.
  - user_id (UUID, FK → users) — The user who reacted.
  - parent_id (UUID)        — ID of the entity being liked (a post ID or comment ID).
                              Note: NO foreign key constraint — see design decisions.
  - parent_type (VARCHAR(10))— Discriminator: 'comment' or 'post'. Enforced by CHECK.
  - status (VARCHAR(10))    — The reaction type: 'None', 'Like', or 'Dislike'.
                              Enforced by CHECK constraint. 'None' represents a
                              withdrawn/neutral reaction (the user removed their like).
  - created_at, updated_at  — Audit timestamps.

Notable: NO deleted_at column. Like the comments table, likes don't use soft delete.

Indexes:

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_likes_user_parent_unique
      ON likes (user_id, parent_id, parent_type);

    Ensures a user can only have one reaction per entity. Combining parent_id and
    parent_type in the unique constraint means a user can like both a post and a
    comment that happen to share the same UUID (extremely unlikely but logically
    correct). When a user changes their reaction, the existing row is updated
    rather than creating a new one.

  CREATE INDEX idx_likes_parent
      ON likes (parent_id, parent_type);

    Composite index for finding all likes on a specific post or comment. Used
    when fetching the "newest likes" to display on a post, or when counting
    likes for a comment.

Trigger:
  Same BEFORE UPDATE trigger pattern for auto-updating updated_at.

Design decisions:
  - POLYMORPHIC ASSOCIATION: The likes table uses a (parent_id, parent_type) pattern
    instead of separate post_likes and comment_likes tables. This is sometimes called
    a "polymorphic association" or "generic foreign key." The advantage is a single
    table for all like operations; the disadvantage is that parent_id cannot have a
    real foreign key constraint to either posts or comments (since it references
    different tables depending on parent_type).
  - CHECK constraints on parent_type and status enforce valid enum values at the
    database level, providing a safety net beyond application-level validation.
  - 'None' status instead of deletion — when a user removes their like, the row is
    updated to status='None' rather than being deleted. This preserves the history
    of the interaction and simplifies the upsert logic (always UPDATE, never
    DELETE + INSERT).


========================================================
CROSS-CUTTING DESIGN PATTERNS
========================================================

1. UUID Primary Keys
   All tables use UUID v4 primary keys generated by gen_random_uuid(). This allows
   IDs to be generated without database round-trips and avoids sequential ID
   enumeration attacks. The trade-off is larger key size (16 bytes vs 4 bytes for
   INTEGER) and slightly worse index locality.

2. Soft Delete Pattern (deleted_at)
   Applied to: users, security_devices, blogs, posts.
   NOT applied to: comments, likes.
   Soft-deleted rows remain in the database but are excluded from queries via
   partial indexes (WHERE deleted_at IS NULL). This enables data recovery and
   audit trails while maintaining the illusion of deletion for the application.

3. Denormalization
   Three instances of intentional denormalization:
   - posts.blog_name      — duplicated from blogs.name
   - posts.likes_count    — materialized count from likes table
   - posts.dislikes_count — materialized count from likes table
   - comments.commentator_user_login — duplicated from users.login
   All trade read performance (no JOINs needed) for write complexity (must keep
   denormalized values in sync).

4. Partial Indexes
   Used extensively on tables with soft delete to:
   - Enforce uniqueness only among active records (users login/email, device_id)
   - Keep indexes small by excluding deleted rows
   - Speed up the most common query pattern (active records only)

5. Automatic Timestamps
   Every table has created_at (set once at INSERT via DEFAULT NOW()) and updated_at
   (refreshed on every UPDATE via the shared trigger function). This provides a
   consistent audit trail across all entities.

6. CHECK Constraints
   Used on the likes table to enforce valid enum values (parent_type, status) at
   the database level. This provides defense-in-depth beyond application validation.

7. IF NOT EXISTS / OR REPLACE
   All CREATE statements use IF NOT EXISTS (tables, indexes) or OR REPLACE
   (function), making the entire script idempotent — safe to run multiple times
   without errors or data loss.
